#Use of drone in the Droning sub - task
***This task is to be performed inside the cage, therefore no regulations are applicable as drones will be used in a fully enclosed and safe environment.***

If you decide to fly the drone outside the competition and outside the cage (testing) follow local  PL/EU UAV flying rules (https://uavcoach.com/drone-laws-in-poland/ ) (laws in EU):
Consider extra insurance (aircraft damage insurance - Aero Casco)


Drone must fly in the right category: (PANSA )
PANSA Drone Tower app - DroneTower is the official mobile app by the Polish Air Navigation Services Agency (PANSA) used to report and coordinate drone flights in Poland. It connects drone pilots directly with air traffic control through digital check-ins and two-way communication.
![Drone clasification EASA](drone clasification EASA.jpg)


Specific category - The “specific” category is intended for medium-risk operations whose flight parameters exceed the “open category”.
(link: https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/domains/drones-air-mobility/operating-drone/specific-category-civil-drones)

Open Category - The “open category” allows for low risk flights. These includes VLOS (Visual Line Of Sight) flights  link ** Most likely category for the ERC competition **

Open category - low risk - civil drones (link: https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/domains/drones-air-mobility/operating-drone/open-category-low-risk-civil-drones)
 
1. Look for a C-class label (on the drone’s physical casing, packaging, or manual)
2. Drones without labels (legacy drones) bought before January 1, 2024 (treated by weight: A1,A2,A3)
3. Modifying a retail drone - or building one yourself - voids any factory C-class rating. Under EASA regulations, your drone will now be classified as a “privately built”.
	
Privately built drone classification: (most likely classification for our drone)
By Maximum Take-Off Mass (MTOM) - total weight of the drone including competition modification, payloads, batteries, etc.
	
If privately built & < 25kg --> A3 : must fly far from people


Operator / Pilot registration

Drones operators and pilot’s main responsibilities:
(https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/light/topics/drone-operators-pilots )

Drone Operator - any person or organization who owns or rents one or more registered drones. Register yourself with your National Aviation Authority.

Drone pilot - the person actually flying the drone without necessarily owning or renting the drone.

A person can be both, the operator and the pilot.
